        <body>
    <h2>Requesting a Special Education Evaluation in District 79</h2>

    <p>District 79 serves students across all five boroughs of New York City, providing educational opportunities in alternative settings. This guide explains how to request a special education evaluation if you believe your child, attending a District 79 program in locations like Brooklyn, the Bronx, Manhattan, Queens, or Staten Island, needs special education services. Understanding your rights under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law is crucial throughout this process.</p>

    <h3>Local Context: District 79 Alternative Programs</h3>

    <p>District 79 focuses on students who benefit from smaller learning environments and specialized support. This includes students in GED programs with IEPs, those attending transfer schools with disabilities, and students in other alternative educational settings. These programs often serve students with a wide range of needs, from emotional and behavioral challenges to learning disabilities and developmental delays. If you think your child's learning needs aren't being met, and they attend a District 79 program, you have the right to request an evaluation. Consider, too, that District 79, while providing alternative pathways, is still bound by the same special education regulations as any other district within NYC. Don't let the "alternative" setting deter you from advocating for appropriate services.</p>

    <h3>The Written Referral Process: Your Starting Point</h3>

    <p>The first step is to submit a written referral to the Committee on Special Education (CSE) Chairperson in District 79. This formal request initiates the evaluation process. Your referral letter should clearly state your concerns about your child's academic, social-emotional, or behavioral development and how these concerns impact their learning. Be specific and provide concrete examples. Avoid generalizations. For example, instead of saying "My child is struggling in reading," write "My child is reading two grade levels below their current grade, has difficulty decoding unfamiliar words, and struggles to comprehend complex sentences, as demonstrated by their performance on classroom assessments and standardized reading tests. This impacts their ability to complete assignments and participate in class discussions." </p>

    <p>Your letter should include your child's full name, date of birth, school, and grade, as well as your contact information. You can obtain the CSE Chairperson's contact information from the school principal or by contacting the District 79 administration. Keep a record of all communication with the school, including the date and time of any phone calls, emails, or meetings. This documentation can be invaluable if you need to escalate your concerns later.</p>

    <p><b>Here's a template for your referral letter:</b></p>
    <pre>
    [Your Name]
    [Your Address]
    [Your Phone Number]
    [Your Email Address]

    [Date]

    Committee on Special Education Chairperson
    District 79
    [Address of District 79 Administration]

    RE: Request for Special Education Evaluation for [Child's Full Name], DOB: [Child's Date of Birth]

    Dear CSE Chairperson,

    I am writing to formally request a special education evaluation for my child, [Child's Full Name], who is currently enrolled in [School Name] in District 79, [Grade Level].

    I am concerned about [Child's Name]'s [specific area of concern, e.g., reading skills, math skills, social-emotional development, behavior]. Specifically, [provide 2-3 specific examples with data if possible. E.g., "My child is struggling with reading comprehension and often misunderstands the meaning of written text. They scored below grade level on the [Name of Assessment] assessment in [Month, Year]. They also struggle to stay focused during reading activities and often become frustrated. This impacts their ability to complete classroom assignments and participate effectively in class discussions"].

    [Optional: Briefly mention any interventions that have already been tried and their results. E.g., "My child has received additional support in reading from the classroom teacher, but their progress has been limited."]

    I believe that a comprehensive special education evaluation is necessary to determine if my child has a disability that is impacting their ability to access the general education curriculum and to identify the appropriate supports and services to meet their unique needs.

    I have attached copies of [list any attached documents, e.g., school records, previous evaluations, teacher notes].

    I look forward to hearing from you soon to discuss this matter further.  Please contact me at [Your Phone Number] or [Your Email Address] to schedule a meeting.

    Sincerely,

    [Your Name]
    </pre>

    <h3>Important: The 60-Day Timeline and Parental Consent</h3>

    <p>Once the CSE receives your written referral, they have 60 school days to complete the evaluation and hold an IEP meeting. This timeline is mandated by New York State Education Law and Part 200 Regulations (8 NYCRR §200.5). This is a *critical* timeframe. It's essential to follow up with the CSE Chairperson if you haven't heard back within a reasonable time (e.g., two weeks after submitting the request). Documentation is key; keep copies of everything you send. If the 60-day timeline is not met, request clarification and consider escalating the matter following NYC Department of Education guidelines.  Document every interaction with the CSE, including dates, times, individuals spoken with, and the content of the conversation.</p>

    <p><b>Crucially, the 60-day timeline *pauses* when the district sends you a consent form for the evaluations.</b> They *cannot* proceed with the evaluations without your informed, written consent (IDEA, 34 CFR §300.300). Review this consent form carefully.  You have the right to agree to some evaluations and refuse others. For instance, if you recently had a private psycho-educational evaluation, you can provide that to the CSE and potentially decline a similar evaluation from the district.  Refusing an evaluation doesn't necessarily stop the process, but it might affect the scope of the evaluation and the information available to the CSE.</p>

    <p><b>Parental Rights and Procedural Safeguards:</b>  At the time of the referral, and again at the IEP meeting, the school must provide you with a copy of your parental rights and procedural safeguards under IDEA (34 CFR §300.504).  Read these carefully!  They outline your rights to participate in all meetings, to examine your child's records, to obtain an independent educational evaluation (IEE) at public expense under certain circumstances, and to challenge the IEP if you disagree with it.  Don't hesitate to ask the CSE Chairperson to explain any part of the rights document that you don't understand.</p>

    <h3>Understanding the Evaluation Process</h3>

    <p>The special education evaluation is a comprehensive assessment of your child's strengths and needs. It typically includes:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><b>Psychological Evaluation:</b> Assesses cognitive abilities, learning style, and emotional functioning.</li>
        <li><b>Educational Evaluation:</b> Evaluates academic skills in reading, writing, and math.</li>
        <li><b>Social History:</b> Gathers information about your child's developmental, medical, and family history. This is usually completed through a parent interview.</li>
        <li><b>Classroom Observation:</b>  A qualified professional observes your child in their classroom setting to assess their behavior, interaction with peers, and learning strategies.</li>
        <li><b>Other Evaluations:</b> Depending on your child's needs, additional evaluations may be conducted, such as a speech and language evaluation, occupational therapy evaluation, or physical therapy evaluation.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs):</b> If you disagree with the results of the school's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)). The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate.  This is a powerful right, and it's important to understand the process for requesting an IEE.  Generally, you need to provide a written notice to the district stating your disagreement with their evaluation and your intention to seek an IEE. The district may have specific providers or a list of approved evaluators, but you are generally entitled to choose a qualified evaluator.</p>

    <h3>Preparing for the IEP Meeting</h3>

    <p>After the evaluations are completed, the CSE will schedule an IEP meeting to discuss the results and develop an Individualized Education Program (IEP) for your child.  This meeting is a critical opportunity to advocate for your child's needs and ensure that the IEP is tailored to their specific learning style and challenges.</p>

    <p><b>Here's how to prepare:</b></p>

    <ol>
        <li><b>Review the Evaluation Reports:</b> Carefully read all the evaluation reports before the meeting. Highlight any areas of concern or questions you have.</li>
        <li><b>Gather Your Own Data:</b>  Collect any data or information that supports your concerns, such as report cards, homework samples, teacher notes, or observations you've made at home.</li>
        <li><b>Define Your Goals:</b> Think about what you want your child to achieve academically, socially, and emotionally.  What specific skills do you want them to develop?</li>
        <li><b>Prepare Questions:</b>  Write down a list of questions you want to ask the CSE team.  Don't be afraid to ask for clarification or challenge assumptions.</li>
        <li><b>Consider Bringing an Advocate:</b>  You have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to the IEP meeting to support you and help you navigate the process.  A knowledgeable advocate can be invaluable in ensuring that your child's rights are protected.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>The IEP: Your Child's Roadmap to Success</h3>

    <p>The IEP is a legally binding document that outlines your child's educational needs, goals, and the supports and services they will receive.  It includes information such as:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><b>Present Levels of Performance (PLOP):</b> A description of your child's current academic, social, and emotional functioning.</li>
        <li><b>Measurable Annual Goals:</b> Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals that your child is expected to achieve within a year.</li>
        <li><b>Special Education and Related Services:</b>  A list of the specific services your child will receive, such as special instruction, speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling, or transportation.</li>
        <li><b>Accommodations and Modifications:</b> Changes to the curriculum or classroom environment that will help your child access the general education curriculum. Accommodations don't change *what* is being taught, but *how* it is taught (e.g., extended time on tests, preferential seating). Modifications change *what* is being taught (e.g., simplified reading materials, reduced workload).</li>
        <li><b>Placement:</b> The educational setting where your child will receive their special education services. This could be a general education classroom with support, a resource room, or a special class. Placement decisions must be based on your child's individual needs and should be made in the least restrictive environment (LRE).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Least Restrictive Environment (LRE):</b> IDEA mandates that children with disabilities be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate (34 CFR §300.114).  The IEP team must consider a range of placement options and ensure that your child is placed in the setting that is most appropriate for their needs while also maximizing their opportunities to interact with non-disabled students.</p>

    <h3>Common Pitfalls and Advocacy Tips</h3>

    <ul>
        <li><b>District Delays:</b> Districts sometimes delay evaluations or IEP meetings.  Stay persistent and document all communication.  If the 60-day timeline is violated, file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED).</li>
        <li><b>Generic IEPs:</b>  Be wary of IEPs that are not tailored to your child's specific needs.  Insist on measurable goals and specific services.</li>
        <li><b>Lack of Parental Involvement:</b>  You are a critical member of the IEP team.  Your input and concerns should be valued and addressed.  If you feel your voice is not being heard, consider bringing an advocate to the meeting.</li>
        <li><b>Inadequate Services:</b>  If you believe your child is not receiving the services they need, request an IEP review meeting.  You can also pursue mediation or file a due process complaint.</li>
        <li><b>Placement Disputes:</b> If you disagree with the proposed placement, you have the right to challenge the decision.  Consider obtaining an IEE to support your position.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>FAQ</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>What if my child is already receiving some support services but doesn't have an IEP?</b></dt>
        <dd>If your child is receiving interventions like Response to Intervention (RTI), but isn't making adequate progress, it's time to request a formal special education evaluation. RTI is *not* a substitute for special education, and it shouldn't be used to delay or deny a necessary evaluation.</dd>

        <dt><b>Can the school refuse to evaluate my child?</b></dt>
        <dd>The school can refuse to evaluate your child if they believe there is no reasonable suspicion that your child has a disability. However, they must provide you with written notice of their refusal and explain the reasons for their decision. You have the right to challenge this decision by requesting a due process hearing.</dd>

        <dt><b>What if I disagree with the IEP?</b></dt>
        <dd>If you disagree with the IEP, you have several options. You can request an IEP review meeting to discuss your concerns and try to reach an agreement. You can also pursue mediation, which is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school resolve your differences. Finally, you can file a due process complaint, which initiates a formal legal process where an impartial hearing officer will make a decision about the IEP.</dd>

       <dt><b>What are "related services"?</b></dt>
       <dd>Related services are support services that are required to assist a child with a disability to benefit from special education (34 CFR §300.34). These services can include, but are not limited to: speech-language pathology and audiology services, interpreting services, psychological services, physical and occupational therapy, recreation (including therapeutic recreation), counseling services (including rehabilitation counseling), orientation and mobility services, and medical services for diagnostic or evaluation purposes. Make sure the IEP clearly specifies the frequency, duration, and location of all related services.</dd>

       <dt><b>What is Prior Written Notice (PWN) and why is it important?</b></dt>
       <dd>Prior Written Notice (PWN) is a written explanation the school district is required to provide you whenever they propose to initiate or change (or refuse to initiate or change) the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child, or the provision of FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education) to your child (34 CFR §300.503). PWN must be provided in your native language and must include a description of the action proposed or refused by the agency; an explanation of why the agency proposes or refuses to take the action; a description of each evaluation procedure, assessment, record, or report the agency used as a basis for the proposed or refused action; a statement that you have protection under the procedural safeguards of Part B of the IDEA; sources for you to contact to obtain assistance in understanding the provisions of Part B of the IDEA; a description of other options that the IEP team considered and the reasons why those options were rejected; and a description of the factors that are relevant to the agency’s proposal or refusal. Always carefully review PWN to understand the district's reasoning and ensure your rights are protected.</dd>
    </dl>

</body>
